  the west but again the worrying signs  seem to trump that right now and it  almost sort of seems like a 50 50 bet as  to which way this is going to go right  now no one actually seems to know but  one thing people are really dialing in  on was this meeting yesterday between  russian president vladimir putin and his  foreign secretary sergey lavrov in which  lavrov sort of gave this highly scripted  briefing  but suggested that the window for  diplomacy has not yet closed those  avenues for de-escalation still exist  okay but as you mentioned uh nato allies  still very concerned with the build-up  of russian troops at the border it seems  like they're preparing for the worst  what's the latest there  yeah we saw that announcement from  canadian officials yesterday of  increased funding and increased lethal  aid to ukraine we're seeing similar  moves from countries around the world  with the option of loan guarantees for  example to prop up the ukrainian  government allow them to keep  functioning we should point out that  financial support is something that  ukraine has said it could really use  right now but we're also seeing those  worrying signs for example the united  states announced announced yesterday  that it is shuttering its embassy in  kiev  sending the few remaining diplomatic  staff who are there to the western part  of ukraine further away from what could  be the front lines if russia proceeds  with an invasion and of course those  dire warnings that the u.s and many nato  allies have given to their own citizens  to leave the country immediately while  it's still possible and the window for  that seems to be closing because of  course uh insurance companies no longer  want to insure aircraft in ukrainian  airspace meaning that the number of  commercial flights available continues  to dwindle by the day  something we will watch in the hours and  days to come thank you so much  jackson jackson proskow in washington  for us this morning
 

